3. Technology Stack
3.1 Backend

Python 3.12+

FastAPI (async API layer)

LangChain (retrieval, tool abstraction, embeddings)

LangGraph (agentic workflow orchestration)

OpenAI Responses API (LLM + tool calling)

PostgreSQL (primary database)

pgvector (vector storage)

Redis (caching + rate limiting)

Celery or equivalent async task queue (document ingestion)

3.2 Database Strategy
Primary Database

PostgreSQL serves as:

Tenant store

User management

OAuth tokens

Conversation logs

Usage tracking

Billing metadata

Embeddings via pgvector

Vector Strategy (Phase 1)

pgvector with IVF index

Multi-vector storage per document

Metadata filtering by tenant

Vector Strategy (Phase 2 – Scale Upgrade)

Migrate heavy retrieval workloads to Qdrant

Keep Postgres for transactional data

3.3 Multimodal RAG Architecture

Vectriva uses a Multi-Vector Retrieval Pattern:

Each document produces multiple vectors:

Text chunks

Table summaries

Image captions

Structured summaries of Excel sheets

Each vector is stored independently but linked to a parent document.

Retrieval Flow:

User query embedding generated

Vector similarity search filtered by tenant

Top-K vectors retrieved

Parent context reconstructed

Context passed to LLM

This ensures:

Fine-grained semantic matching

Accurate table retrieval

Image-aware answers

Reduced hallucination

3.4 Agentic Layer

LangGraph orchestrates deterministic state transitions.

The agent is not a free-form loop. It is a structured graph.

